1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:13,866
In February of 2030, four separate arms of the
United States government arrived at the

2
00:00:13,886 --> 00:00:19,953
same question in the same month, which is the
closest that government ever comes to an

3
00:00:19,973 --> 00:00:20,520
opinion.

4
00:00:20,920 --> 00:00:26,796
The Federal Trade Commission wanted to know how a
company founded in 2026 had acquired

5
00:00:26,816 --> 00:00:32,829
eleven competitors in thirty-one months without a
single merger review going against it.

6
00:00:33,229 --> 00:00:38,823
The Securities and Exchange Commission wanted to
know how a balance sheet could carry

7
00:00:38,843 --> 00:00:44,700
two hundred and seventeen billion dollars of
property, plant and equipment with a capital

8
00:00:44,720 --> 00:00:49,719
expenditure line that had never in four years
exceeded nine hundred million.

9
00:00:50,119 --> 00:00:56,055
The Government Accountability Office, at the request
of a subcommittee, wanted to know

10
00:00:56,075 --> 00:01:02,564
why forty-one per cent of the nation's clinical
laboratory capacity now ran on the instruments

11
00:01:02,584 --> 00:01:03,949
of one private firm.

12
00:01:04,349 --> 00:01:10,244
And in a field office in Philadelphia, SSA Owen
Carrick — whose file was two years old,

13
00:01:10,264 --> 00:01:16,091
whose supervisor had twice told him to close it, and
who had a wall in a spare room at

14
00:01:16,111 --> 00:01:21,530
home with nine photographs on it — wanted to know
where the buildings came from.

15
00:01:21,930 --> 00:01:27,896
Umbrella's general counsel recommended the standard
posture: cooperate narrowly, produce

16
00:01:27,916 --> 00:01:32,250
what is compelled, object to everything
else, and run the clock.

17
00:01:32,650 --> 00:01:38,492
Pell recommended it. Catherine recommended it.
Sixteen lawyers at four hundred dollars

18
00:01:38,512 --> 00:01:40,060
an hour recommended it.

19
00:01:40,579 --> 00:01:43,230
No. Give them everything.

20
00:01:43,629 --> 00:01:47,717
Director — with respect, you cannot mean
*everything*. In an inquiry of this breadth,

21
00:01:47,737 --> 00:01:49,070
unrestricted production is —

22
00:01:49,471 --> 00:01:55,141
Mr. Ferris. How many documents does this
company generate in a year?

23
00:01:55,540 --> 00:01:58,421
Approximately — I would estimate
four hundred million.

24
00:01:58,822 --> 00:02:03,753
And how many people will the Government
Accountability Office assign?

25
00:02:04,153 --> 00:02:05,754
Eleven. Perhaps fourteen.

26
00:02:06,153 --> 00:02:12,222
Then give them four hundred million documents, and
give them a floor of this building

27
00:02:12,242 --> 00:02:14,943
to read them in, and give them coffee.

28
00:02:15,343 --> 00:02:21,841
The narrator wishes to be clear that this was not
obstruction by volume, which is a lawyer's

29
00:02:21,861 --> 00:02:28,004
trick and which any competent investigator sees
through in a week. Wesker did something

30
00:02:28,024 --> 00:02:29,349
far more dangerous.

31
00:02:29,750 --> 00:02:31,859
He gave them a *research assistant*.

32
00:02:32,379 --> 00:02:39,385
They will have a full-text index, a liaison who
answers within the hour, and the right

33
00:02:39,405 --> 00:02:47,064
to walk into any Umbrella facility in the United States
without notice. Not a tour. Unescorted

34
00:02:47,084 --> 00:02:49,270
access, any door, any time.

35
00:02:49,669 --> 00:02:50,759
Any door.

36
00:02:51,159 --> 00:02:58,168
Every door that exists on a plan. Sublevel B4 is
not on a plan. Arklay's lower levels

37
00:02:58,188 --> 00:03:05,196
are not on a plan. If they ask for something I have
not given them, they will get it,

38
00:03:05,216 --> 00:03:12,556
because the only thing that would ever hurt this
company is an investigator who discovers

39
00:03:12,576 --> 00:03:14,209
he has been managed.

40
00:03:14,610 --> 00:03:20,886
The GAO's team leader was a woman named Denise
Ferrand, fifty-four years old, twenty-nine

41
00:03:20,906 --> 00:03:27,253
years in federal audit, three previous reviews that
had ended careers. She had been chosen

42
00:03:27,273 --> 00:03:33,337
because she could not be charmed, and the narrator
would like to record that she never

43
00:03:33,357 --> 00:03:33,620
was.

44
00:03:34,020 --> 00:03:40,008
She arrived on the second of March expecting a fight
and was given a floor, a badge, a

45
00:03:40,028 --> 00:03:46,017
parking space and a liaison named Mr. Pell who
answered every question in a flat voice

46
00:03:46,037 --> 00:03:49,930
within forty minutes, whatever it
was, at whatever hour.

47
00:03:50,330 --> 00:03:54,199
She spent six months there. She was
extremely good at her job.

48
00:03:54,599 --> 00:04:00,093
She found: five instances of aggressive transfer
pricing through the Irish subsidiary,

49
00:04:00,113 --> 00:04:05,798
all legal, two of them barely. A pattern of
acquisitions structured to fall four per cent

50
00:04:05,818 --> 00:04:11,376
below merger-review thresholds. A contract with the
state of Delaware that a reasonable

51
00:04:11,396 --> 00:04:17,017
person would call a sweetheart deal. Nine hundred
million dollars of political donations

52
00:04:17,037 --> 00:04:22,466
routed through a foundation. And an internal
culture that she described, in the final

53
00:04:22,486 --> 00:04:25,094
report, in a sentence that became famous:

54
00:04:25,615 --> 00:04:31,126
The company's employees display a uniformity of
purpose and an absence of internal dissent

55
00:04:31,146 --> 00:04:36,474
that the review team found without precedent in
twenty-nine years of federal audit, and

56
00:04:36,494 --> 00:04:39,732
which the company attributes to its
hiring practices.

57
00:04:40,132 --> 00:04:45,631
She did not find the plant that had appeared
overnight in Newark, because the plant's

58
00:04:45,651 --> 00:04:49,462
paperwork went back to 2011 and the
county recorder agreed.

59
00:04:49,862 --> 00:04:55,669
She did not find Sublevel B4, because she never asked
for a floor that no drawing showed,

60
00:04:55,689 --> 00:05:01,562
and because Gus — asked directly, in a corridor, in
July, whether there was anything under

61
00:05:01,582 --> 00:05:07,258
the garage — looked her in the eye and said, *Just
dirt, ma'am, and about nine miles of

62
00:05:07,278 --> 00:05:13,020
conduit*, and was telling the truth as he understood
it, because Gus had never been told

63
00:05:13,040 --> 00:05:15,312
that the fourth button was strange.

64
00:05:15,711 --> 00:05:22,638
And she did not find Arklay at all, because Arklay was
a private residence in Pennsylvania

65
00:05:22,658 --> 00:05:26,651
belonging to a subsidiary that was
not under review.

66
00:05:27,052 --> 00:05:32,701
On her last evening, Denise Ferrand asked for a
meeting with the Director, and got one

67
00:05:32,721 --> 00:05:33,822
in seven minutes.

68
00:05:34,341 --> 00:05:37,271
I want to ask you something that isn't
going in the report.

69
00:05:37,672 --> 00:05:38,762
Then ask it.

70
00:05:39,161 --> 00:05:44,010
In twenty-nine years I have never had unrestricted
access. Not once. Not from a bank,

71
00:05:44,030 --> 00:05:48,935
not from a defence contractor, not from a hospital
system that was killing people. And

72
00:05:48,955 --> 00:05:53,918
I have sat in that building for six months and been
given absolutely everything I asked

73
00:05:53,938 --> 00:05:59,072
for, immediately, and I have found a list of ordinary
corporate sins that will make eleven

74
00:05:59,092 --> 00:06:00,847
pages of a report nobody reads.

75
00:06:01,127 --> 00:06:05,652
So either you are the cleanest large company in the
history of this country, or you gave

76
00:06:05,672 --> 00:06:09,009
me everything because you knew what I
would not think to ask for.

77
00:06:09,408 --> 00:06:15,038
Wesker regarded her with what was, by his
standards, genuine respect.

78
00:06:15,559 --> 00:06:17,229
Which do you think it is?

79
00:06:17,629 --> 00:06:22,264
(flat) I think I have been handled by an expert,
and I think I cannot prove it, and I

80
00:06:22,284 --> 00:06:26,372
think that is the most frightening
professional experience I have ever had.

81
00:06:26,774 --> 00:06:33,559
Ms. Ferrand, may I give you something? Not an
inducement — you will notice I have not

82
00:06:33,579 --> 00:06:39,084
offered you anything in six months, which I
imagine you also noticed.

83
00:06:39,483 --> 00:06:40,993
I noticed on day four.

84
00:06:41,394 --> 00:06:48,719
Then take this instead: you are right. You have not
been given everything. No one is ever

85
00:06:48,739 --> 00:06:55,900
given everything, by anyone, ever, and the belief
that one *can* be is the foundational

86
00:06:55,920 --> 00:06:57,963
error of your profession.

87
00:06:58,574 --> 00:07:04,675
But you have been given more than any auditor in
this country has ever been given by a

88
00:07:04,695 --> 00:07:10,867
company this size, and everything you were given is
true. If that is not enough for the

89
00:07:10,887 --> 00:07:17,202
conclusion you wanted, the shortfall is not in my
disclosure. It is in the questions your

90
00:07:17,222 --> 00:07:19,124
mandate permits you to ask.

91
00:07:19,524 --> 00:07:22,334
Denise Ferrand looked at him for
a long moment.

92
00:07:22,853 --> 00:07:26,283
That's the most honest thing anyone has
said to me in this building.

93
00:07:26,684 --> 00:07:32,614
It is the most honest thing anyone will say to
you in any building. Goodnight.

94
00:07:33,014 --> 00:07:38,266
The narrator promised you Gus in a corridor and
owes you the whole of it, because the

95
00:07:38,286 --> 00:07:41,244
review turned on it and neither
party ever knew.

96
00:07:41,644 --> 00:07:47,263
Denise Ferrand had been in that building for four
months and had developed the habit that

97
00:07:47,283 --> 00:07:52,713
every good auditor develops, which is to ask the
questions in corridors rather than in

98
00:07:52,733 --> 00:07:53,093
rooms.

99
00:07:53,613 --> 00:07:55,839
Mr. Gus. How long have you worked here?

100
00:07:56,240 --> 00:08:00,060
Since the building went up, ma'am.
Nineteen ninety-eight.

101
00:08:00,459 --> 00:08:02,444
And the garage. Three levels?

102
00:08:02,845 --> 00:08:04,075
Three, ma'am.

103
00:08:04,475 --> 00:08:05,945
Anything under it?

104
00:08:06,785 --> 00:08:10,811
Just dirt, ma'am, and about nine
miles of conduit.

105
00:08:11,209 --> 00:08:17,106
He was telling the truth. That is the thing that has
to be understood about that exchange

106
00:08:17,126 --> 00:08:22,889
and about the entire four hundred million pages that
Umbrella handed over: Gus had been

107
00:08:22,909 --> 00:08:28,540
down to Sublevel B4 four thousand times. He had a
key to it on the ring in his pocket

108
00:08:28,560 --> 00:08:34,256
while he was speaking. He serviced its air handler
monthly, including the one that was

109
00:08:34,276 --> 00:08:35,519
not an air handler.

110
00:08:35,919 --> 00:08:41,177
And he had never been told that any of it was
unusual, and nobody had ever instructed

111
00:08:41,197 --> 00:08:46,579
him to conceal it, and so when a federal auditor
asked him a question in a corridor his

112
00:08:46,599 --> 00:08:51,981
mind produced the answer that four hundred people in
that building would have produced,

113
00:08:52,001 --> 00:08:57,259
which is that the garage has three levels and there
is dirt under it, because that is

114
00:08:57,279 --> 00:09:01,419
what is on the drawings and Gus was a man
who worked from drawings.

115
00:09:01,820 --> 00:09:07,455
The narrator notes that if Denise Ferrand had asked
*how many buttons are in the lift*,

116
00:09:07,475 --> 00:09:13,565
she would have had the entire thing in about nine
seconds, and that she has said so, publicly,

117
00:09:13,585 --> 00:09:18,830
twice, and that she describes it as the great
regret of a thirty-one-year career.

118
00:09:19,350 --> 00:09:20,360
Thank you.

119
00:09:20,759 --> 00:09:26,625
Ma'am. You want anything found in this building, you
ask me. I know where everything is.

120
00:09:27,025 --> 00:09:30,466
He meant it. That is the worst part. He
meant it completely.

121
00:09:30,865 --> 00:09:37,076
The report was published on the fourteenth of
October: one hundred and forty-one pages,

122
00:09:37,096 --> 00:09:41,086
twenty-two findings, four
recommendations, no referrals.

123
00:09:41,486 --> 00:09:47,330
It concluded that Umbrella Life Sciences had
engaged in aggressive but lawful tax and

124
00:09:47,350 --> 00:09:53,401
acquisition structuring, that its dominance of
clinical diagnostics presented a national

125
00:09:53,421 --> 00:09:59,334
resilience risk in the event of a corporate failure,
and that Congress should consider

126
00:09:59,354 --> 00:10:02,576
strategic stockpiling of
alternative platforms.

127
00:10:02,976 --> 00:10:09,151
In other words: the danger identified by the United
States government was that Umbrella

128
00:10:09,171 --> 00:10:10,647
might one day *stop*.

129
00:10:11,047 --> 00:10:13,836
The stock rose four per cent on
publication.

130
00:10:14,356 --> 00:10:18,976
(broadcast) — the most exhaustive corporate review in
a decade, and the headline finding

131
00:10:18,996 --> 00:10:23,616
is that we're too dependent on them. Umbrella's
response this morning was a single line:

132
00:10:23,636 --> 00:10:28,467
the company welcomes the report and has already begun
implementing all four recommendations.

133
00:10:28,866 --> 00:10:32,057
(broadcast) Which is presumably a
nightmare for their competitors —

134
00:10:32,456 --> 00:10:35,567
(broadcast) Their competitors are the
four recommendations.

135
00:10:36,596 --> 00:10:44,960
Subjugation, institutional. Federal review concluded
with no adverse finding. Public trust

136
00:10:44,980 --> 00:10:53,156
increased. Regulatory risk reduced for approximately
four years. One million one hundred

137
00:10:53,176 --> 00:10:58,373
thousand Points. Level fifty.
Intelligence tab unlocked.

138
00:10:59,592 --> 00:11:07,145
Intelligence. Dossier on any living person: four
thousand each. Asset Network, one nation:

139
00:11:07,165 --> 00:11:14,297
nine hundred thousand. Signals Access, commercial:
two million four hundred thousand.

140
00:11:14,317 --> 00:11:21,616
Counter-Intelligence Cell: one million eight hundred
thousand. What you learn here, you

141
00:11:21,636 --> 00:11:22,879
cannot unlearn.

142
00:11:23,469 --> 00:11:28,639
He bought one dossier. Four thousand Points,
against a balance of three million.

143
00:11:29,038 --> 00:11:35,345
Not the GAO team leader. Not the senator whose
subcommittee had commissioned the review.

144
00:11:35,365 --> 00:11:41,600
Not any of the sixteen lawyers, or the four
competitors, or the journalist who had been

145
00:11:41,620 --> 00:11:44,188
calling Pell every week since Basel.

146
00:11:44,589 --> 00:11:50,895
He bought a dossier on a man whose name had appeared
exactly twice in four hundred million

147
00:11:50,915 --> 00:11:57,081
documents, both times on a visitor log, both times
signing in to a meeting with somebody

148
00:11:57,101 --> 00:11:57,433
else.

149
00:11:58,582 --> 00:12:06,700
Dossier. Sloane, Martin J. Central Intelligence
Agency, Directorate of Operations. Current

150
00:12:06,720 --> 00:12:14,566
assignment: technology acquisition, special
activities. Age fifty-four. Beirut, Warsaw,

151
00:12:14,586 --> 00:12:22,433
Islamabad, Caracas. He has read every public
document your company has produced. He has

152
00:12:22,453 --> 00:12:26,954
not written anything down about
you in four years.

153
00:12:27,984 --> 00:12:29,294
Four years.

154
00:12:29,693 --> 00:12:30,864
Four years.

155
00:12:31,264 --> 00:12:39,292
The Belgian operation was fifteen months ago. Basel
was sixteen. He started before any

156
00:12:39,312 --> 00:12:39,854
of it.

157
00:12:40,254 --> 00:12:43,850
He started the week you filed
your first patent.

158
00:12:44,250 --> 00:12:50,176
Albert Wesker sat back in the chair and, for the
first time since a boardroom in Trenton,

159
00:12:50,196 --> 00:12:54,250
felt something that he would have
refused to call excitement.

160
00:12:54,650 --> 00:12:57,821
The voice in his skull chose that
moment to arrive.

161
00:12:58,341 --> 00:12:59,559
A hunter.

162
00:12:59,957 --> 00:13:06,568
A *professional*. Everyone else who has come at this
company has come with a mandate and

163
00:13:06,588 --> 00:13:13,124
a form to fill in. This one has spent four years
reading, has told no one, and has left

164
00:13:13,144 --> 00:13:18,248
nothing in writing that could be taken away
from him by a committee.

165
00:13:18,648 --> 00:13:20,537
He is dangerous.

166
00:13:20,937 --> 00:13:27,733
He is *useful*. A man who writes nothing down has
already decided that his institution

167
00:13:27,753 --> 00:13:34,469
is not equal to the problem. That is not an enemy,
Adolf. That is a man waiting to be

168
00:13:34,489 --> 00:13:36,768
offered a better arrangement.

169
00:13:37,168 --> 00:13:38,498
He closed the panel.

170
00:13:39,018 --> 00:13:45,613
Mr. Pell. Find out where Martin Sloane drinks, and
do not approach him. I want to be in

171
00:13:45,633 --> 00:13:49,948
the room by accident, and I want it
to take about a year.

172
00:13:50,347 --> 00:13:51,788
It took fifteen months.
